{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Perhaps there are fifty righteous within the city.</b> Rashi wrote: there were five cities, ten for each and every city. 'Destroy' — for five: nine for each and every city, and you are the righteous one of the world — join with them. So too: perhaps there will be found there forty, and four shall escape; and so thirty — three shall escape; and so twenty — two; and ten — one. And for fewer than ten he did not ask, because a generation cannot endure with fewer than ten righteous, and therefore the generation of the Flood was destroyed because there were not ten there, for indeed those who entered the ark were only eight: Noah and his wife, his three sons and his three daughters-in-law. Furthermore, Abraham reckoned that there were ten in Sodom: Lot and his wife, his four daughters and his four sons-in-law. And when there were not ten there, they were destroyed just as the generation of the Flood was destroyed when there were not ten there to plead for the generation. This is what is written: 'And HaShem went' — immediately after He mentioned the ten — for since the Holy One, blessed be He, knew that there were not ten there and it was impossible for Abraham to reduce further from them, therefore the Shekhinah withdrew. And the multiplication of prayer by Abraham each and every time — after the Holy One, blessed be He, conceded to him to bear the whole place if there be found there forty-five, and forty — four cities shall escape — it was a known thing that thirty would save three, and twenty — two, and ten — one city, all according to that reckoning, so why should Abraham trouble himself with words to multiply prayer for nothing? But Abraham reasoned that the desire of HaShem is to have mercy upon the many more than upon the few, for the few do not have as much merit — as they said of blessed memory: 'Those who are few and do the Torah are not like those who are many and do the Torah.' And behold, the Holy One, blessed be He, conceded to him each and every time that He would have mercy even upon the few; and this is why He mentioned the attribute of mercy at the beginning of the matter. And the reason for the change in expression — once 'I will not destroy' and once 'I will not do': at first He said 'I will not destroy' when He would find there forty-five — meaning, they are worthy of destruction since there are not ten for each city, but I will not destroy them, only I will strike them with sufferings. Afterward He said 'I will not do for the sake of forty' — meaning, I will not do — neither destruction nor sufferings — since they are ten for each city. And so He said of thirty: 'I will not do,' because three cities are the majority; but of twenty, and likewise of ten, He said 'I will not destroy' — meaning, but they shall be stricken with sufferings, since the three, which are the majority, are the wicked. And since there were not ten there, all were destroyed. And from this passage there is a great rousing to the reckoning of ten, which is the endurance of the world — from what has been explained here: the destruction of the world on account of the lack of ten."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:13:33.356625177Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}